$\begin{align}
a\cdot a^7&=a^1\cdot a^7
\\\\
&=a^{1+7}
\\\\
&=a^{8}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $x^m\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
a\cdot a^7&=\underbrace{a}_\text{1 time}\cdot\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a \cdot a}_\text{7 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a}_\text{8 times}
\\\\
&=a^{8}
\end{align}$
